# Dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# Build output
/.next/
/out/
/build
/coverage
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Environment. `.env.example` is the documented list of keys and is committed;
# nothing else here is. `old.txt` holds the credentials the Django build used,
# moved aside at the cutover, and contains real secrets.
.env*
!.env.example
old.txt

# Deployment
.vercel

# Cloudflare build artefacts, on a branch that cannot produce them.
#
# Nothing here deploys to Cloudflare -- that work lives on
# `cloudflare-workers-deployment` -- but build directories outlive a branch
# switch, and `.open-next/` is ~69MB of bundled Worker and assets. Without these
# rules, checking main out after building that branch presents the whole tree as
# untracked, which is how a build directory gets committed by accident.
#
# `.dev.vars` is listed separately because the `.env*` rule above does not reach
# it, and it holds real secrets.
.open-next/
.wrangler/
.dev.vars
.dev.vars.*

# Editors and OS
.DS_Store
*.pem
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# Database dumps.
#
# This used to be a blanket `*.json`, added so a `manage.py dumpdata` backup
# could never be committed by accident. That rule outlived its reason and cost
# more than it saved: every JSON file that genuinely belonged in the repo had to
# be un-ignored by name, nothing warned when one was missed, and `package.json`
# and `package-lock.json` sat untracked for the project's whole life because of
# it -- which is how the Tailwind build drifted two minor versions without
# anyone noticing. Django is gone and so is `dumpdata`; what is left is named
# directly.
*.dump.json
*.dumpdata.json
*.dump.sql

# Working notes, not the record.
#
# Design specs and plans written while working on a change: the reasoning that
# matters ends up in the commit messages, CLAUDE.md and MIGRATION.md, and
# leaving these here put a directory in the repo with nothing in it for anyone
# browsing the project. `.claude/` also carries per-machine settings.
docs/superpowers/
plan/
.claude/
.vscode/
text.txt

# Pages saved from somewhere else, kept to read data off.
#
# Named directly rather than as `*.html`, the same bargain the dumps above
# make: a blanket rule is the one nobody notices excluding a file that
# belonged here.
#
# The reason these cannot simply sit untracked is Tailwind. It walks every
# non-gitignored file and treats any word that parses as a class name as one in
# use, so a page saved from another site brings that site's class vocabulary
# into this build -- see the `@source not` lines in `app/globals.css` and
# `scripts/check-css-sources.mjs`, which exist because prose merely *naming* a
# utility was enough to emit it.
certifications.html

# Python leftovers from the Django build, until the virtualenv is removed.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
